WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.228
Small project-specific wrappers can make unsafe API migration more maintainable.

2
00:00:05.228 --> 00:00:16.729
When many call sites need the same buffer checks, truncation policy, path validation, or return-value handling, a wrapper can centralize the rule and make future review easier.

3
00:00:16.729 --> 00:00:19.800
A good wrapper makes the contract more visible.

4
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:29.079
It should expose destination capacity, current used length where relevant, ownership expectations, truncation behavior, and failure reporting.

5
00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:37.705
The caller should be able to tell whether the operation completed, whether data was truncated, and who owns any returned allocation.

6
00:00:37.705 --> 00:00:41.887
A wrapper that hides errors can be worse than the original call.

7
00:00:41.887 --> 00:00:51.558
If it silently truncates data, swallows conversion failures, or returns a partial result without a clear status, it creates a false sense of safety.

8
00:00:51.558 --> 00:00:55.283
The purpose of a wrapper is not to make errors disappear.

9
00:00:55.283 --> 00:00:58.616
It is to make errors consistent and hard to ignore.

10
00:00:58.616 --> 00:01:04.693
Structured buffer types can help keep pointer, capacity, used length, and ownership together.

11
00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:11.228
They reduce the chance that a function receives a pointer from one buffer and the size from another.

12
00:01:11.228 --> 00:01:17.632
They also make repeated operations easier to reason about because the same fields travel together.

13
00:01:17.632 --> 00:01:21.683
Helpers should stay simple enough for reviewers to understand.

14
00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:27.434
A large reusable framework can become another opaque layer, especially in legacy C code.

15
00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:39.392
For most migrations, small focused helpers are better: one for bounded formatting, one for validated numeric conversion, one for safe file creation, or one for buffer append behavior.

16
00:01:39.392 --> 00:01:42.006
The wrapper design should include tests.

17
00:01:42.006 --> 00:01:50.697
Normal input, boundary input, truncation, allocation failure where practical, invalid arguments, and cleanup paths should be covered.

18
00:01:50.697 --> 00:01:56.774
A wrapper becomes trustworthy when its contract is documented, tested, and used consistently.
